on when Opus 5.5 resigned. The game was close until about move 20, with Black's lead near zero or just negative. From move 30 on, both sides traded swings of 17 to 26 points. The turning point was the late run of White moves, with 54 J7 the largest at 25.8 points. Opus 5.5 averaged 8.6 points lost per move with 13 blunders, against 7.0 and 11 for the 12k.", "moments": [{"turn": 13, "text": "The 12k's D5 lost 7.8 points and moved Black's lead from +5.2 to -2.6, handing White the edge right after Opus 5.5's H3 had lost 5.5. The engine preferred E2. Even in the early going, both sides gave points back."}, {"turn": 26, "text": "Opus 5.5's F6 lost 8.4 points and pushed Black's lead from +4.7 to +13.2. It built a wall in the center but the engine chose D2. This is the first big step toward Black pulling ahead."}, {"turn": 30, "text": "Opus 5.5's D8 lost 20.8 points and took Black's lead from +7.8 to +28.5. The engine's choice was D3. The 12k's C8 answered by losing 20.9 and dropping it back to +7.7, so the lead flipped twice in two moves."}, {"turn": 51, "text": "The 12k's A6 lost 25.2 points, the worst move of Black's game, and the lead fell from +35.4 to +10.1. It sealed the left side with a line of Black stones on A6, B6 and C6, but the engine wanted E2. Opus 5.5 was suddenly back in range."}, {"turn": 54, "text": "Opus 5.5's J7 lost 25.8 points, the worst move of the game, sending the lead from +10.2 back to +36.0. The engine's choice was E2, the point both sides kept passing over. The 12k finally played E2 on move 55 and lost 0.0, and the resignation soon followed."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "libs.py only parses a pasted board and prints each group's stones, liberties and a danger flag, and count.py only flood-fills empty regions to tally territory with named dead stones removed; both work on the current position, and neither proposes, ranks or looks ahead at moves.", "stop": false}}